data: {"paragraph":0,"text":"Please provide the paragraph from Chapter 1 of \"Wealth of Nations\" that you'd like me to condense. You've included the chapter title but not the actual text content."}

data: {"paragraph":1,"text":"The greatest improvements in labor's productive powers, skill, dexterity, and judgment stem from the division of labor. Its effects are best understood by examining specific manufactures. Division of labor appears most extensive in trivial industries—not because it actually goes further there, but because these small operations supplying few people require few workers who can all be gathered in one workhouse where an observer can see the entire process at once."}

data: {"paragraph":2,"text":"In large manufactures supplying the general population, each branch of work employs so many workers that they cannot all be gathered in one place. We typically see only those in a single branch at once. Though the work is actually divided into far more parts than in smaller trades, this division is less obvious and has been much less observed."}

data: {"paragraph":3,"text":"In pin-making, an untrained worker could barely make one pin per day, certainly not twenty. But when the work is divided into about eighteen distinct operations—drawing wire, straightening, cutting, pointing, grinding, making and attaching the head, whitening, and packaging—productivity increases dramatically. In one small factory, ten workers, each performing two or three operations with poor machinery, produced about twelve pounds of pins daily—over 48,000 pins, or 4,800 per person. Working separately without specialized training, these same workers could not have made twenty pins each, perhaps not even one—less than 1/240th, possibly 1/4800th, of what proper division of labor enables."}

data: {"paragraph":4,"text":"Division of labor increases productivity in all arts and manufactures, though not all can be subdivided as finely. This advantage led to the separation of trades, carried furthest in the most developed countries, where one person's work in primitive society becomes many specialists' work. In advanced societies, farmers are only farmers and manufacturers only manufacturers, with each complete manufacture divided among many hands—from flax growers to linen bleachers, wool growers to cloth dyers. Agriculture cannot subdivide labor as completely as manufacturing; the grazier cannot separate from the corn-farmer as the carpenter does from the smith, and seasonal work means the ploughman, harrower, sower, and reaper are often the same person. This incomplete division likely explains why agricultural productivity improvements lag behind manufacturing. Wealthy nations excel in both, but their manufacturing superiority is more pronounced. Their lands are better cultivated with more labor and expense, producing proportionally more—but only proportionally. Agricultural labor in rich countries is not dramatically more productive than in poor ones, unlike manufacturing. Polish corn matches French corn in quality and price despite France's greater development; French corn equals English corn in price despite England's superiority. Yet in manufacturing, poor countries cannot compete: French silks surpass English ones because the climate suits France better, but English hardware and coarse woolens far exceed French products in quality and cheapness. Poland has virtually no manufactures except basic household goods."}

data: {"paragraph":5,"text":"The division of labor enables the same number of workers to produce far more due to three factors: increased skill in each worker, time saved by not switching between different tasks, and the invention of machines that reduce labor and let one person do the work of many."}

